Z||000026||fyerstarter||04-26-2001||11:11 PM||||I'm sure we could feed the homeless but why? Why can't the homeless just get a job like everyone else and feed themselves?<P>In this booming economy it is not hard to find a job. When I got out of high school I went down to Big O Tires and got a ****  job paying $5.50 an hour. There were no requirements, I didn't have to know anything, I didn't have to dress up for the interview, I didn't even have to wait for a return phone call. I walked in and 10 mins later I was working.<P>I hated that job and I worked my ass off 11 hours a day just so that I could support myself. I see no reason why anyone else is not capable of the same.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Jake_Dragon||04-27-2001||07:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>Jake, that sounds very southern of ya   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Southern by the Grace of Mopar?<BR>I moved here (Florida) in 1984 from Ohio. Drove down in a 73 dodge charger, man I miss that car. Life has its way of working out. In 97 my home of 13 years burned to the ground, I totaled my 72 Camaro and my wife totaled her car. I was unable to finish school and had to take a year off. I was seriously depressed. I just put my mind to work on my problems. We bought a new home, I finished school with honors and I am now doing quite well. All I am saying is you cant be responsible for the world take care of yourself and the rest will fall into place.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Ken Wittlief||04-30-2001||09:41 AM||||I took my family to NYC for the first time this last friday.<P>  We did the statue of liberty thing, took the ferry over and just walked around (line was too long to go up inside it).<P>  On the ferry ride back my wife and kids were a little chilled, so I stood up on the bow of the boat as we headed back. The ferry swings out wide and heads into the harbor, on the same channel that steamers would have taken 100 years ago, bringing people over from Europe.<P>  My grandparents came to the US from Germany when they were both 18 (about 100 years ago). Standing on the bow of that ferry, with the bright blue sky over NYC, and the chilled wind in my face, it was like going back in time. I can imagine what it was like for grandpa Oscar, leaving behind everything and everyone he ever knew in germany, and bringing his new wife to America, the loss he must have felt, the doubts, and the hopes and dreams.<P>  After a long ocean voyage like that I think coming into NY harbor would be the hardest part, the stepping off point, you take your wifes hand and walk down that gangplank into a totally unknown world. I can imaging the responsibility and fear he must have felt.<P>   But then, just as you are entering the harbor, there is that giant statue, holding up its lamp. That statue is there for you! That lamp is there to guide you! She is there to welcome you!<P>   It changes the whole situation, from arriving in a huge, cold, and unknown port, to knowing you have made the right decision, that you have come to a better place, and there is room and there is opportunity for you here.<P>   I have to tell you, Im a 43 year old guy, and I was a little choked up on the deck of that ferry saturday afternoon. It was an awesome experience, and in a way I am glad my family had stayed inside the cabin and I had a few moments to myself to experience it alone.<P>   Grandpa worked as a milkman on a horse drawn buggy. His son was in Pattons third army at the battle of the buldge, and later went to night school to become an electrical technician. His son (me) was the first person in our family history to graduate from a university (BSEE from SUNY Buffalo). And I am now making close to $100k a year as an engineer. My daughter is going to Rochester Institute of Technology and wants to be a computer engineer. <P>  grandpa died in 1969. I wish he were here so I could thank him for taking the risk. I wish I could show him the good that has come from it. And I will do all I can to make the best of the opportunity that is available to me here.<P>  In the long run I think that is all any of us can do. Asess your situation, choose your best options, make a general plan, and then make the best out of whatever life has given you.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Galen||04-30-2001||07:07 PM||||Jake, I'll get myself straight one day. I'd have to say overall my displeasure with the world is a one sided one. Not everyone is that bad, just as not everyone is good. I just looked at it as everyone was bad. my bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe.. My brother just sunk a lil under 3500$ in a 65 Mustang to buy & get it running and everything(from my dad). the whol two years I've had my car, I've spent less than 1grand on it besides insurance. Kinda makes me feel blessed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Everything will be good, I used to tell myself that alot. I stopped believing that a while back. Even though I kept telling myself it would. "Take a moment and step back, notice the comedy in life, and laugh" my ideals of life.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||1
